# main is production

Every push to `main` deploys machiilabs.com. There is no staging step between `main` and the public.

## Never, without the user's explicit words for this exact action

- Push, merge, fast-forward, cherry-pick, rebase, or revert anything on `main`.
- "commit", "push", "ship the app", "release Skagway", or finishing a task is **not** permission to touch `main`. Only "merge <branch> to main", "push to main", or "ship the site" counts, and it covers that one action.
- Force-push or reset `main`, ever, unless the user asks for that by name.

If you believe something should go to `main`, stop and ask. Say which branch and exactly what it changes on the live site.

## Every new branch starts from origin/main

```bash
git fetch origin main
git switch -c <name> origin/main
```

- Never branch from whatever is checked out, and never from a release branch such as `skagway-1.3.0` or `flasher-mac-1.4.0`. That is how the 1.3.0 site shipped early: a pond-guide branch was cut from `skagway-1.3.0`, then fast-forwarded onto `main`, carrying every unreleased 1.3.0 page with it.
- Before starting unrelated work, run `git status -sb` and check the current branch.
- Check a branch before proposing it for `main`: `git log --oneline origin/main..<branch>` must list only that task's commits.

## Release branches (`<product>-X.Y.Z`)

Every product the site offers for download gets the same treatment. Products: `skagway`, `flasher-mac` (15CE Flasher for Mac), `flasher-win` (15CE Flasher for Windows). A release branch is named `<product>-X.Y.Z`, for example `skagway-1.3.0` or `flasher-mac-1.4.0`.

- It holds that release's unreleased site copy, release notes, manual changes, and download metadata.
- It merges to `main` only when the user says to ship that release's site, after the signed build exists and the site has its real version, build, filename, and SHA-256 (see `skagway-site-versioning.mdc` for Skagway; the Flasher page and guide for Flasher).
- Keep it current by merging `origin/main` into it, never the other way round.
- To ship: open a pull request into `main` and add the label `ship:<product>-X.Y.Z`. The user merges it on GitHub.

## If unreleased work reaches main anyway

Stop and tell the user. Do not improvise fixes on `main`.

Reverting it on `main` leaves the release commits in `main`'s history, so a later merge of the release branch brings back nothing. After any revert, rebuild the release branch on top of the new `main` by re-applying its full diff, and verify that `git diff origin/main <release-branch>` is exactly the unreleased work.

## Guards

- **GitHub ruleset "Protect main"**: nobody can push to `main` directly or force-push it. Everything reaches `main` through a pull request the user merges.
- **`scripts/check-production-release.sh`** runs on every pull request into `main` (the "Production release check" workflow) and fails it when the pull request:
  - carries commits from any `<product>-X.Y.Z` release branch without the label `ship:<product>-X.Y.Z`
  - changes any product's public download (version, build, SHA-256, filename, URL) or the Skagway coming-soon promo without that label
  - has a download whose build, SHA-256, filename, and URL disagree, or a non-numeric build
- Add a `ship:` label only after the user explicitly says to ship that release. A red check means stop and tell the user, not relabel.
- **`scripts/git-hooks/pre-push`** runs the same check on local pushes to `main` (needs `MACHII_MAIN_PUSH=yes` and `MACHII_SHIP="<product>-X.Y.Z"`). Install once per clone with `bash scripts/install-git-hooks.sh`. Never use `--no-verify`.
- The hook and the workflow both run the copy of the check that is on `main`, so a branch cannot weaken its own check.
